I felt like my insides had entirely  liquefied and just fell out of my body  and it was like that for weeks and  actually months after that so I actually  had two different situations where I was  pregnant and had an abortion the first  time was my freshman year in college it  was my first time taking birth control  and I just wasn't taking it properly  I've always kind of had an issue with  like hormones anything like that and  after trying several birth controls my  body just kind of went haywire I got to  the point where mentally it just wasn't  healthy for me anymore the second time I  was taking birth control the same time  every single day and I ended up getting  pregnant me and my fiancee at the time  decided to start like just using condoms  we weren't very consistent with it so we  just thought like pull out method and I  got pregnant anyway during both  situations I was with a partner it was  actually the same partner during the  first time that I was pregnant we were  dating when I found out that I was  pregnant it was as a result of an  ultrasound at a Gynaecologists previous  pregnancy scares what my mom had always  told me is that your nipples would be  really really sore I had gotten up and I  went straight to the shower and I could  not have water running directly on my  chest I was absolutely terrified the  second time was actually shortly after  we were married I missed my period I was  completely shocked the very first thing  that came to my mind was I can't do this  I was full-time school I had no health  insurance  and I mean we had no money I had no idea  how my anxiety meds would affect a baby  is terrifying I kind of knew even before  taking the test that if it were positive  I would have to get an abortion and it  wasn't really like a decision-making  process for me it was more talking my  partner into it he really did want to  keep the baby and I think ultimately he  did end up presenting that a little bit  the first abortion that I  walking into that abortion clinic was  scary it wasn't a welcoming environment  my partner wasn't able to come into the  room with me even just the room that I  was in itself felt really cold the day I  found out I was pregnant I had called  Planned Parenthood and I made an  appointment and I went in that day and  they told me my due date and my due date  was actually the day that we were  supposed to be getting married  they had me schedule my abortion date  and the next appointment was a week from  that day which fell on my birthday so  that was rough I didn't have a great  sense of exactly what to expect in terms  of the procedure the doctor buzzed into  the room he spoke really quickly didn't  give me the opportunity to actually  speak or respond back I just remember  him saying how are you doing what do you  like to do oh I know one thing you like  to do they have a little paper med cup  put just a pill in it and a glass of  water and they told me that I would just  take that pill and it'd be one of two  pills and it would begin the abortion  and then I'd be free to go I was you  know there by myself with this pill that  would change my life I was given general  anesthesia when I woke up later I could  tell that there were a lot of other  women around me who were in pain it was  the most excruciating pain I have ever  felt I felt like it was kind of a  penance for me when I got home I felt a  general sense of achiness  my body felt tired it was like menstrual  cramps times a thousand  it was like my insides were spasming  when I had my second abortion my partner  was by my side the entire time there was  no general anesthesia it was a gentle  aspiration method and afterwards I  actually had the option to be able to  see the fetus that was removed from my  body what I saw was a collection of  cells it looked like a jellyfish it was  this little pink spongy mass on a petri  dish this wasn't a viable life-form  outside of my own body  afterwards one of the things that I was  told was that I couldn't lift anything  heavy several days after I actually had  the abortion I was in a situation where  I had to carry something it wasn't even  that heavy maybe ten pounds later on  that evening I started bleeding I went  to the bathroom immediately and it was  like all of my insides fell out I don't  know how anybody could bleed that much  and live and it was like that for weeks  and actually months after that it became  really quickly apparent that the amount  that I was bleeding was abnormal and I  called the abortion clinic they were  able to send medication over to a  pharmacy the bleeding lasted for several  hours and once I was able to take the  medication very quickly after that it  stopped what I wasn't prepared for was  some of the emotional aftermath I was  basically just in my room for weeks and  weeks and weeks I started drinking  pretty heavily I couldn't stop thinking  about what if I hadn't made that  decision was absolute torture the  drinking got completely out of control  I felt a sense of depression I could not  be around my friends I could not go  anywhere without knowing that I would be  able to be drunk  my only source of support was my partner  we broke up about two months before the  wedding date and I had completely pushed  him out of my life like he was pretty  much just a roommate I felt like I  didn't fight hard enough for my baby and  then he didn't fight hard enough for me  once my wedding date came around I ended  up in a bathtub with a bottle of Jack  Daniel's crying too like the song that  we were supposed to dance to on our  wedding day in my wedding dress the very  dramatic and kind of ridiculous looking  back on it but at the time I felt like  it was the most poetic thing to do on  that day looking back on those decisions  I feel even more confident that they  were the right choice for me at that  time I don't have any regrets about  making the decision the only regret I  have was that I didn't reach out to  other people to talk about it because  that left me feeling really isolated I'm  still not completely back to myself if  I'm being completely honest physically  it kind of made my cramps worse I  believe more heavily during my periods  it's like every time my period would  come I'd be reminded of that whole  procedure I'm remarried I'm in a  relationship where I'm thinking about  having a child I realized that the same  concerns and the same thoughts that I  had then are still there every year on  that date I kind of put it a little bit  of time aside to kind of sit with myself  and think like this would have been my  baby's birthday they would have been  this old you know I'll write a letter in  my diary to the baby you know whatever  therapeutic thing I can do for myself  after getting sober I met my husband and  we're expecting our first child now I  know none of that would have happened  had I not made the decision that I did  being able to raise a child as  tremendously  difficult I know that now as a woman in  my 30 is that even when you're ready you  never feel like you're completely ready  to be carrying a child after choosing  not to carry another I feel that  apologetic you know weaves kind of come  with me that like I'm choosing to keep  this one and I didn't choose to keep the  other one and it's almost like I have to  apologize to like the idea of my first  baby I can't carry that guilt into this  pregnancy I didn't know what to expect  in the aftermath of an abortion I didn't  realize that I potentially could be  awash in emotions that were a result of  the hormones in my body  it doesn't necessarily get better it  just gets different your life will  change forever regardless of what  decision you make it's just learning to  have a different life after that that's  really important  [Music]
 

